<header><p class="st-kicker">principality · Poland, Lithuania and Russia</p><h1 class="st-title">Principality of Novgorod-Seversky</h1><p class="st-meta">1097 CE – 1523 CE (approx.) · capital Novgorod-Seversky</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A frontier principality of the Chernigov lands, home of Prince Igor, whose failed campaign against the Cumans inspired a great epic.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/novhorod-siverskyi">Novgorod-Seversky</a> on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/desna-river">Desna</a> was an old Severian town. At the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/council-of-liubech">Lyubech conference</a> of 1097 it was assigned to the sons of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sviatoslav-ii-of-kiev">Sviatoslav II of Chernigov</a>, and it became the seat of a junior branch of the Olgovichi, second only to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chernigov">Chernigov</a> itself in their lands.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Its princes held a frontier zone exposed to the steppe and fought the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cumans">Cumans</a> as often as they allied with them. In 1185 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/igor-svyatoslavich">Prince Igor</a> Sviatoslavich led a raid against the Cumans that ended in his defeat and capture, the subject of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/the-tale-of-igors-campaign">Lay of Igor's Campaign</a>. Later princes included <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dmitry-korybut">Dmitry Koribut</a>, a son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/algirdas">Algirdas</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>After the Mongol invasion the land passed to the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/grand-duchy-of-lithuania">Grand Duchy of Lithuania</a> in the 14th century. In 1500 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vasily-shemyachich">Vasily Shemyachich</a> transferred his allegiance to the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/grand-duchy-of-moscow">Grand Duchy of Moscow</a>, which kept the territory by the truce of 1503. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vasily-iii-of-moscow">Vasily III</a> arrested Shemyachich in 1523 and abolished the principality.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/principality-of-novgorod-seversky.jpg" alt="Principality of Novgorod-Seversky" width="320" height="265"><figcaption>Image: Koryakov Yuri · CC BY-SA 2.5 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kievan_Rus%27_1113-1194_-_Novgorod-Seversk_and_Chernigov.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1097 CE – 1523 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/11th-century">11th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>principality</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Novgorod-Seversky</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Igor Sviatoslavich, Dmitry Koribut, Vasily Shemyachich</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Eastern Orthodox Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Old East Slavic</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/ukraine">Ukraine</a>, <a href="/countries/russia">Russia</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Novhorod-Siverskyi Principality, Seversk Principality</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1310&amp;focus=principality-of-novgorod-seversky" aria-label="Principality of Novgorod-Seversky on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5430 381 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Principality of Novgorod-Seversky lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5750 637L5724 651M5750 637L6352 637M5750 637L5556 562M5750 637L5820 533"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5724 651h0M6352 637h0M5556 562h0M5820 533h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5750 637h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5750 637h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=1310&amp;focus=principality-of-novgorod-seversky">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
